{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'defn segment-bytes-match\\\\?|defn parse-segment-address|defn canonical-bytes|defn decode' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | sed -n '300,365p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | sed -n '1521,1553p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '130,178p'\"","aggregated_output":"src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1524:(defn decode-snapshot\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1541:(defn decode\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:279:(defn canonical-bytes\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:319:(defn parse-segment-address\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:398:(defn segment-bytes-match?\n   300\t\n   301\t(defn segment-key\n   302\t  \"Mint the content-addressed key for an opaque payload, derived solely from\n   303\t   the payload: :segment/<algorithm-id>-<digest>.\n   304\t   The 1-arg form defaults to default-hash-algorithm (:blake3).\n   305\t   The 2-arg form accepts {:algorithm :sha256} (or any registered algorithm).\"\n   306\t  ([v]\n   307\t   (segment-key v {:algorithm default-hash-algorithm}))\n   308\t  ([v opts]\n   309\t   (let [algo (clojure.core/get opts :algorithm default-hash-algorithm)\n   310\t         reg (clojure.core/get registry algo)]\n   311\t     (when-not reg\n   312\t       (throw (ex-info (str \"unsupported hash algorithm: \" algo)\n   313\t                       {:algorithm algo})))\n   314\t     (let [algo-id (:address-id reg)\n   315\t           digest (content-hash v {:algorithm algo})]\n   316\t       (keyword \"segment\" (str algo-id \"-\" digest))))))\n   317\t\n   318\t\n   319\t(defn parse-segment-address\n   320\t  \"The authoritative address parser: requires a `segment` namespace keyword,\n   321\t   splits the name on the first `-` into algorithm identifier and digest,\n   322\t   looks the identifier up by exact match (no prefix matching), validates\n   323\t   digest length from the registry entry and lowercase-hex text, and\n   324\t   reconstructs the canonical address to reject alternative spellings.\n   325\t   Returns {:algorithm :<algo>, :digest \\\"<hex>\\\", :canonical :segment/...}\n   326\t   or nil on any rejection (never throws).\"\n   327\t  [address]\n   328\t  (when (keyword? address)\n   329\t    (when (= \"segment\" (namespace address))\n   330\t      (let [n (name address)\n   331\t            i (str/index-of n \"-\")]\n   332\t        (when (and i (pos? i))\n   333\t          (let [algo-id (subs n 0 i)\n   334\t                digest (subs n (inc i))]\n   335\t            (when (re-matches algorithm-id-pattern algo-id)\n   336\t              (when-let [algo (clojure.core/get registry-by-address-id algo-id)]\n   337\t                (let [{:keys [digest-bytes]}\n   338\t                      (clojure.core/get registry algo)]\n   339\t                  (when (and (= (* 2 digest-bytes) (count digest))\n   340\t                             (re-matches hex-digit-pattern digest))\n   341\t                    (let [canonical (keyword \"segment\"\n   342\t                                             (str algo-id \"-\" digest))]\n   343\t                      (when (= canonical address)\n   344\t                        {:algorithm algo,\n   345\t                         :digest digest,\n   346\t                         :canonical canonical}))))))))))))\n   347\t\n   348\t\n   349\t(defn segment-address?\n   350\t  \"True when x is a valid content address in the closed registry:\n   351\t   :segment/<algorithm>-<digest-hex>.\"\n   352\t  [x]\n   353\t  (boolean (parse-segment-address x)))\n   354\t\n   355\t\n   356\t(defn segment-algorithm\n   357\t  \"The algorithm keyword (:blake3, :sha256) carried by a segment address.\n   358\t   Throws on invalid input.\"\n   359\t  [address]\n   360\t  (if-let [parsed (parse-segment-address address)]\n   361\t    (:algorithm parsed)\n   362\t    (throw (ex-info \"not a segment content address\" {:address address}))))\n   363\t\n   364\t\n   365\t(defn segment-digest\n  1521\t          (refuse :unknown-tag (str \"tag \" raw)))))))\n  1522\t\n  1523\t\n  1524\t(defn decode-snapshot\n  1525\t  \"The value of one accepted Jing payload (byte[], Uint8Array or\n  1526\t   Uint8List) that already passed ingress: bytes a backend stored after\n  1527\t   `decode` accepted them, or bytes this codec encoded itself. Refuses\n  1528\t   malformed CBOR, trailing data and anything outside the closed profile,\n  1529\t   but does not re-encode the value to prove canonicality -- that check\n  1530\t   ran once at ingress (docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md, Layering and\n  1531\t   interfaces). Returned byte strings are fresh copies.\"\n  1532\t  [bs]\n  1533\t  (when (zero? (blen bs))\n  1534\t    (refuse :malformed-cbor \"empty input\"))\n  1535\t  (let [[item pos] (read-item bs 0 1)]\n  1536\t    (when (not= pos (blen bs))\n  1537\t      (refuse :trailing-data (str (- (blen bs) pos) \" bytes after the item\")))\n  1538\t    (item->value bs item)))\n  1539\t\n  1540\t\n  1541\t(defn decode\n  1542\t  \"The value of one canonical Jing payload (byte[], Uint8Array or\n  1543\t   Uint8List): the ingress check. Refuses everything `decode-snapshot`\n  1544\t   refuses and also any payload whose value does not re-encode to exactly\n  1545\t   these bytes. Returned byte strings are fresh copies.\"\n  1546\t  [bs]\n  1547\t  (let [value (decode-snapshot bs)]\n  1548\t    (when-not (bytes= bs (encode value))\n  1549\t      (refuse :non-canonical))\n  1550\t    value))\n   130\t\n   131\t## 2. Governing invariants\n   132\t\n   133\tEvery rule below derives from `datom.world.md`. The derivations are named\n   134\tonce here so later sections can cite them.\n   135\t\n   136\t**I1. Code at rest is content in `dao.jing`.** An image, a row, a vector,\n   137\ta manifest, a derivation record: each is an opaque value at its\n   138\t`segment-key`. `dao.jing` assigns identity and retrieval and nothing else\n   139\t(`dao.jing.md`, *Definition*). The linker never asks the store what a\n   140\tvalue means.\n   141\t\n   142\t**I2. Code in motion is a stream in `dao.stream`.** A request for code, a\n   143\tpayload answering it, a refusal, an absence, a timeout: each is a value\n   144\tat a position in an append-only sequence. No function call crosses a host\n   145\tboundary (`datom.world.md`, *Host Boundaries*). Consequently every value\n   146\ton the link or content streams is plain data that survives the host\n   147\tcodec unchanged: no function, handle, or ambient state (`dao.stream.md`,\n   148\t*Envelopes*).\n   149\t\n   150\t**I3. Location is not identity.** Whether the content is in this process,\n   151\tin a file, behind a socket, or on a DHT peer changes how bytes are\n   152\tobtained, not what the linker checks (`dao.jing.md`, *Reads*). A local\n   153\tcomposition and a remote one differ in the creation specification of the\n   154\tstreams they wire, and in nothing the linker can observe.\n   155\t\n   156\t**I4. No operation waits.** The portable linker interface is a step that\n   157\treturns what is true now, including \"not yet\" (`dao.stream.md`, OD-5). A\n   158\twaiting `fetch` may wrap the step as host policy on hosts that can afford\n   159\tit and is never the interface another interpreter is written against.\n   160\t\n   161\t**I5. Verification is address-directed and identity-directed, and both\n   162\trun.** The storage address and the format identity have different\n   163\tpreimages for two of the four formats and the same preimage for the other\n   164\ttwo; in every case both checks execute, because an index entry is a claim\n   165\tand never a proof (`yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` section 6; D14). Every\n   166\tcheck is directed by the algorithm the address or identity carries; the\n   167\tlinker never re-mints under a default and compares with `=` (Class 3 of\n   168\t`dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`).\n   169\t\n   170\t**I6. The VM never invokes a loader.** Absence of code is a park plus a\n   171\trequest emission; the loaded image arrives as a value in VM state\n   172\t(`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 7.2). The linker is an observer on a\n   173\trequest stream, not a function the kernel calls.\n   174\t\n   175\t**I7. The registry is a value.** `yin.vm.module` already keeps the\n   176\tregistry as a map in VM state. This document keeps that and changes what\n   177\tthe map holds: manifests, derivation records, portable slices, and\n   178\tper-task bindings lowered from them, produced by an evaluation the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
